# pstars-and-stripes.ti59 Program that demonstrates interrupting the printer while printing a row.
# From Magatzine "TI PPC NOTES", V6N4-5P8, 1981
#
# These are the original notes in the article:

# STARS AND STRIPES - by Richard Snow.
# According to Richard it was written only as a
# demonstration of the graphics capability of the PC100, using the
# newly discovered pseudo-code which interrupts the printing in the middle of a line.
# Since the initialization process is somewhat complex, and must be accomplished
# trom the keyboard, Richard included label E to list all the necessary steps. Thus,
# when you press E to start things rolling, you'll see the listing as shown on the left.
# Pressing finally A will start the plotting. The ADV is just there to be accomplished
# after the plotting so you can tear off your beautiful drawing and dazzle your friends
# with it.
#
# The pseudo instructions is quite interesting and touchy, remarks Richard. ANY USER
# CALLS TO A LABEL PAST THE PSEUDO CODE SEEMS TO PUT THE CALCULATOR INTO AN ENDLESS
# LOOP. (Never-never land!) That is the reason why in all programs, and especially in
# PLOT 60, all the user labels are so close to the beginning. But you may call direct
# addresses without punishment.
#
# One interesting feature of the pseudo-code is that if OP 05 is NOT used, the
# buffer contents of the PC100 (A,B,C or D) printer are printed. Example: Press
#   pi PRT CLS OP 00 SBR 023
# after initialization of any Graphics Mode program. It is surprising to see "pi"
# printed a second time when it no longer exists in the TI-59 memory (the OP 00 took
# care of that) nor in the display (it was wiped out by he CLR). OP 05 messages may
# be printed out a second time as well this way.


#MODEL: TI-59
SOLID-STATE-MODULE: ML
PRINTER: on

# For compactness, the program is only here as key codes. If you need the listing,
# load the program and LIST it on the printer.
PROGRAM:
92 76 11 61 00 33 76 16 43 09  69 01 69 02 69 03 69 04 76 17
25 69 05 68 74 80 00 00 00 00  00 00 06 42 14 16 43 10 69 01
52 69 02 25 69 03 02 05 69 04  17 04 42 00 73 00 69 03 69 30
73 00 69 04 17 97 00 00 55 08  97 14 00 53 04 00 42 00 00 69
03 02 05 69 04 17 43 11 69 02  43 09 69 03 69 04 17 43 12 69
02 43 13 69 03 43 10 69 04 17  97 00 01 09 16 98 92 76 15 43
15 69 01 43 16 69 02 43 17 69  03 43 18 69 04 69 05 98 01 00
69 17 90 25 61 00 24 36 19 71  00 45 37 31 46 31 81 25 11 98

REGISTERS:
01 = 60006025    #  ↑ ↑J
02 = 6000600060  # ↑ ↑ ↑
03 = 51005125    #  * *J
04 = 5100510051  # * * *
05 = 6000600025  # ↑ ↑ J
06 = 60006000    #  ↑ ↑
07 = 5100510025  # * * J
08 = 51005100    #  * *
09 = 6464646464  # =====
10 = 2605002685  # K4 K4
11 = 26050064    #  K4 =
12 = 26050026    #  K4 K
13 = 500260500   # 4 K4
14 = 6           #
15 = 1731371735  # ENTER
16 = 21353230    #  FROM
17 = 26174514    # KEYB
18 = 3213351662  # OARD⇄

# The printout could be fully automated here, but in spirit of the original author's intent, we print the instructions
# instead and then let the user follow them.
KEYSTROKES:
15  E
Wait: 5s